/-!
# An algebraic index-one DVR extension with perfect residue field

Source: Stacks Project, More on Algebra, Tag 09II,
proposition-epp-essentially-finite-type, p-base preparation paragraph
(blueprint: lem-sfb-dvr-perfect-residue-algebraic-tower).
-/

@[expose] public section

set_option autoImplicit false

universe u v

namespace IsDiscreteValuationRing

/-- An arbitrary DVR of positive residue characteristic admits an algebraic fraction-field
extension whose integral closure is a DVR with unchanged uniformizer and perfect purely
inseparable residue field. Both the field and its integral closure are directed unions of
finite stages with the same maximal-ideal extension property.

Source: Stacks Project, More on Algebra, Tag 09II,
proposition-epp-essentially-finite-type, p-base preparation paragraph
(blueprint: lem-sfb-dvr-perfect-residue-algebraic-tower).

Proof:
1. Choose a uniformizer pi and a p-basis of the residue field by
   lem-weil-pbc-p-basis-exists. Lift the p-basis to elements x_i of A. In an algebraic
   closure, provided by lem-pc-algebraic-closure-exists-unique and
   def-weil-algebraic-closure, choose compatible roots alpha_(i,n) with
   alpha_(i,n+1)^p = alpha_(i,n) and alpha_(i,0) = x_i.
2. For finite J and n >= 1, the algebra B_(J,n) defined by T_i^(p^n) = x_i is finite
   free on the restricted monomials by lem-weil-pbc-monic-quotient-free. Its reduction
   modulo pi is a field by lem-sfb-p-independent-root-quotient-field, and every element
   of that reduction has its p^n-th power in the original residue field. Empty J gives A.
3. By lem-sfb-finite-free-field-fibre-dvr, B_(J,n) is a DVR with uniformizer pi. Evaluation
   at the chosen roots embeds it into the algebraic closure: a nonzero element is
   u*pi^a by lem-weil-pbc-dvr-unit-times-uniformizer-power, and stays nonzero there.
4. The stages increase with J and n. Their inclusions are local: the decomposition
   u*pi^a shows that pi times a later stage contracts to pi times the earlier stage.
   Finite unions of J and maxima of n provide common upper bounds.
5. Take the directed union A* of these rings and the union P of their fraction fields.
   Each fraction field is B_(J,n)[1/pi], finite over K by the monomial basis. Thus P/K
   is algebraic. Every nonzero element of A* is u*pi^a, and pi remains a nonunit since
   an inverse would occur at a finite stage. Every nonzero ideal is generated by the
   least power of pi occurring in it. Hence A* is a DVR and m_A A* = pi A* = m_A*.
6. Each finite stage is integral over A by lem-weil-pbc-finite-algebra-is-integral
   and integrally closed in its fraction field by lem-weil-pbc-pid-noetherian-normal.
   A monic equation over A is also one over that stage, identifying it with the integral
   closure of A in its fraction field. The same argument identifies A* with the integral
   closure of A in P, giving the asserted union of integral closures.
7. The contraction calculation identifies the residue field of A* with the union of
   the stage residue fields. Every residue element has a p-power in the base field,
   proving pure inseparability and algebraicity. Write a base residue element in the
   p-basis expansion as a sum of c_E^p*b^E, and use the sum of
   c_E*alpha_(i,1)^E to obtain its p-th root. For any stage residue element, take roots
   of its finitely many base coefficients and replace alpha_(i,n) by alpha_(i,n+1).
   Frobenius preserves finite sums and products, proving perfection. No completeness or finite
   p-basis is used. -/
theorem exists_algebraic_perfect_residue_tower
    (A : Type u) [CommRing A] [IsDomain A] [IsDiscreteValuationRing A]
    (K : Type v) [Field K] [Algebra A K] [IsFractionRing A K]
    (p : ℕ) (hp : 0 < p) [CharP (IsLocalRing.ResidueField A) p] :
    ∃ (P : Type (max u v)) (_ : Field P) (_ : Algebra K P) (_ : Algebra A P)
      (_ : IsScalarTower A K P),
      Algebra.IsAlgebraic K P ∧
      ∃ hC : IsDiscreteValuationRing (integralClosure A P),
        ∃ (_ : IsLocalHom (algebraMap A (integralClosure A P))),
          (IsLocalRing.maximalIdeal A).map (algebraMap A (integralClosure A P)) =
            @IsLocalRing.maximalIdeal (integralClosure A P) _ hC.toIsLocalRing ∧
          PerfectField (IsLocalRing.ResidueField (integralClosure A P)) ∧
          @Algebra.IsAlgebraic (IsLocalRing.ResidueField A)
            (IsLocalRing.ResidueField (integralClosure A P)) _ _
            (IsLocalRing.ResidueField.map (algebraMap A (integralClosure A P))).toAlgebra ∧
          @IsPurelyInseparable (IsLocalRing.ResidueField A)
            (IsLocalRing.ResidueField (integralClosure A P)) _ _
            (IsLocalRing.ResidueField.map (algebraMap A (integralClosure A P))).toAlgebra ∧
          ∃ (I : Type (max u v)) (_ : Nonempty I) (F : I → IntermediateField K P),
            Directed (· ≤ ·) F ∧
            (∀ j : I, Module.Finite K (F j)) ∧
            (∀ x : P, ∃ j : I, x ∈ F j) ∧
            (∀ j : I, ∃ hj : IsDiscreteValuationRing (integralClosure A (F j)),
              (IsLocalRing.maximalIdeal A).map (algebraMap A (integralClosure A (F j))) =
                @IsLocalRing.maximalIdeal (integralClosure A (F j)) _ hj.toIsLocalRing) ∧
            (∀ x : P, x ∈ integralClosure A P ↔
              ∃ (j : I) (y : integralClosure A (F j)),
                algebraMap (F j) P (y : F j) = x) := by
  sorry

end IsDiscreteValuationRing
